d languages that is powerful, adaptable, and efficient enough to run locally, even on a phone. \u003c/em\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eToday, Cohere Labs, Cohere’s research arm, is introducing \u003cstrong\u003eTiny Aya\u003c/strong\u003e, the most capable multilingual open-weight model at its scale. Tiny Aya delivers state-of-the-art translation quality, strong multilingual understanding, top-quality target language responses, and broad language coverage in a model small enough to run locally, even on consumer hardware and mobile phones. No barriers to experimentation, just a powerful family of models that can run anywhere.\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eMultilingual AI has made rapid progress, but performance and usability still concentrate around a small set of dominant languages and large-scale infrastructure. Tiny Aya takes a different approach: combining efficient design with deep multilingual research to support balanced performance across languages while remaining practical to deploy and adapt. Instead of shallow coverage across hundreds of languages, Tiny Aya emphasizes meaningful multilingual depth enabling researchers, developers, and communities to build AI that reflects their own linguistic and cultural contexts.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003eWhat We're Releasing\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eTinyAya-Base, a pretrained 3.35B-parameter model:\u003c/strong\u003e TinyAya-Base covers 70+ languages*, including many lower-resourced languages from around the globe.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eTinyAya-Global, a powerful instruction-tuned multilingual model: \u003c/strong\u003eBuilt on top of TinyAya-Base, TinyAya-Global delivers strong, balanced performance across 67 supported languages. It serves as the default truly multilingual system, ideal for applications that require consistent quality across diverse linguistic settings in a single deployment.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eA family of specialized instruction-tuned models\u003c/strong\u003e: Alongside TinyAya-Global, we’re introducing specialized variants that deepen performance within specific linguistic regions while maintaining strong multilingual capability more broadly. This structure combines shared cross-lingual learning with targeted regional depth giving researchers and builders flexibility in how they deploy multilingual AI (details of the model variants and regional specializations are described below).\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eA new massively multilingual fine-tuning dataset and benchmarks:\u003c/strong\u003e Covering multiple domains, languages, and tasks, these resources provide a foundation for systematic multilingual experimentation, enabling reproducible evaluation and continued exploration of data-centric training strategies.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.11510\" rel=\"noreferrer\"\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cu\u003eA detailed technical report\u003c/u\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e:\u003c/strong\u003e Sharing the research, training strategy, and evaluation insights behind Tiny Aya.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cfigure class=\"kg-card kg-image-card kg-card-hascaption\"\u003e\u003cimg src=\"https://storage.ghost.io/c/81/47/8147eb50-617d-4929-b563-3922b88421fd/content/images/2026/02/data-src-image-b618a357-5f7f-462d-be5b-e65f9af76247.png\" class=\"kg-image\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1600\" height=\"900\" srcset=\"https://storage.ghost.io/c/81/47/8147eb50-617d-4929-b563-3922b88421fd/content/images/size/w600/2026/02/data-src-image-b618a357-5f7f-462d-be5b-e65f9af76247.png 600w, https://storage.ghost.io/c/81/47/8147eb50-617d-4929-b563-3922b88421fd/content/images/size/w1000/2026/02/data-src-image-b618a357-5f7f-462d-be5b-e65f9af76247.png 1000w, https://storage.ghost.io/c/81/47/8147eb50-617d-4929-b563-3922b88421fd/content/images/2026/02/data-src-image-b618a357-5f7f-462d-be5b-e65f9af76247.png 1600w\" sizes=\"(min-width: 720px) 720px\"\u003e\u003cfigcaption\u003e\u003ci\u003e\u003cem class=\"italic\" style=\"white-space: pre-wrap;\"\u003eThe instruction-tuned models from the Tiny Aya family perform competitively with existing massively multilingual models at this scale. Aggregating benchmark performance on our focus languages across multiple tasks (translation, language understanding, mathematical reasoning, and open-ended generations on both technical and non-technical domains), we find that Tiny Aya is advancing the state-of-the-art in generative multilingual AI at this scale across all languages from West Asia and Africa.\u003c/em\u003e\u003c/i\u003e\u003c/figcaption\u003e\u003c/figure\u003e\u003cp\u003eTiny Aya represents a shift in how we think about how multilingual systems are built and shared. With efficient design, deep research into multilingual pretraining and post-training, and a focus on linguistic diversity from the ground up, Tiny Aya brings high-quality AI closer to the people who need it most: researchers working on underrepresented languages, developers building locally, and communities shaping technology on their own terms. For example, a university lab in India could deploy Tiny Aya as an offline translation or AI education tool in classrooms and community settings without relying on cloud APIs.\u0026nbsp;\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\u003cfigure class=\"kg-card kg-image-card kg-card-hascaption\"\u003e\u003cimg src=\"https://storage.ghost.io/c/81/47/8147eb50-617d-4929-b563-3922b88421fd/content/images/2026/02/data-src-image-52d676f9-9622-49f7-80af-e66f3940baa3.png\" class=\"kg-image\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1600\" height=\"900\" srcset=\"https://storage.ghost.io/c/81/47/8147eb50-617d-4929-b563-3922b88421fd/content/images/size/w600/2026/02/data-src-image-52d676f9-9622-49f7-80af-e66f3940baa3.png 600w, https://storage.ghost.io/c/81/47/8147eb50-617d-4929-b563-3922b88421fd/content/images/size/w1000/2026/02/data-src-image-52d676f9-9622-49f7-80af-e66f3940baa3.png 1000w, https://storage.ghost.io/c/81/47/8147eb50-617d-4929-b563-3922b88421fd/content/images/2026/02/data-src-image-52d676f9-9622-49f7-80af-e66f3940baa3.png 1600w\" sizes=\"(min-width: 720px) 720px\"\u003e\u003cfigcaption\u003e\u003ci\u003e\u003cem class=\"italic\" style=\"white-space: pre-wrap;\"\u003eThe dominance of languages on the web, here measured by page counts in CommonCrawl, typically steers the performance across languages in multilingual LLMs. Tiny Aya maintains stable performance even for languages that are under-represented on the web (the right end of this graph), and advances their inclusion in multilingual AI.\u003c/em\u003e\u003c/i\u003e\u003c/figcaption\u003e\u003c/figure\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eTechnical Innovation\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eTiny Aya is grounded in several years of research from the Aya initiative on how multilingual models can scale responsibly. In particular, the training approach builds on our recent research around \u003ca href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.10766\"\u003e\u003cu\u003eincreasing language plasticity through tokenization\u003c/u\u003e\u003c/a\u003e, \u003ca href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.19806\"\u003e\u003cu\u003eincreasing naturalization of synthetic data\u003c/u\u003e\u003c/a\u003e, \u003ca href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00931\"\u003e\u003cu\u003esmart fusion of diverse generations\u003c/u\u003e\u003c/a\u003e and \u003ca href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.09473\"\u003e\u003cu\u003etargeted selection of merging methods\u003c/u\u003e\u003c/a\u003e. Together, these methods allow multilingual signals to be combined while preserving linguistic nuance and strengthening language-specific structure.\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eAnother core design principle behind Tiny Aya was efficiency under realistic compute budgets. By completing post-training on a single 64 NVIDIA H100 GPU cluster, we demonstrate that careful multilingual data design and training strategy can substitute for brute-force scaling. These ideas shaped Tiny Aya from the ground up, guiding the tokenizer design, the data mixture, and the way we approached specialization across language clusters.\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eTiny Aya moves beyond multilinguality as a uniform objective and incorporates strategies that preserve diversity during training while enabling efficient downstream adaptation. This design allows researchers to reshape the model without fighting against rigid alignment or over-specialized post-training, making it easier to fine-tune for new domains, emerging languages, or community-driven evaluation frameworks.\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\u003cfigure class=\"kg-card kg-image-card kg-card-hascaption\"\u003e\u003cimg src=\"https://storage.ghost.io/c/81/47/8147eb50-617d-4929-b563-3922b88421fd/content/images/2026/02/data-src-image-bc0354e0-53d5-4ed9-98ab-6eff98b4b590.png\" class=\"kg-image\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1600\" height=\"900\" srcset=\"https://storage.ghost.io/c/81/47/8147eb50-617d-4929-b563-3922b88421fd/content/images/size/w600/2026/02/data-src-image-bc0354e0-53d5-4ed9-98ab-6eff98b4b590.png 600w, https://storage.ghost.io/c/81/47/8147eb50-617d-4929-b563-3922b88421fd/content/images/size/w1000/2026/02/data-src-image-bc0354e0-53d5-4ed9-98ab-6eff98b4b590.png 1000w, https://storage.ghost.io/c/81/47/8147eb50-617d-4929-b563-3922b88421fd/content/images/2026/02/data-src-image-bc0354e0-53d5-4ed9-98ab-6eff98b4b590.png 1600w\" sizes=\"(min-width: 720px) 720px\"\u003e\u003cfigcaption\u003e\u003ci\u003e\u003cem class=\"italic\" style=\"white-space: pre-wrap;\"\u003eOpen-ended generation scores in relation to model size. Tiny Aya demonstrates improved generation quality relative to previous Aya models while operating at a smaller scale, and outperforms strong multilingual baselines such as Gemma at comparable parameter counts.\u003c/em\u003e\u003c/i\u003e\u003c/figcaption\u003e\u003c/figure\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSmall Models, Strong Performance\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eWe approached building Tiny Aya with a broader goal in mind: building models that remain reliable across many languages while staying efficient enough for local use. The result is a family of models that remain competitive with existing multilingual models across a range of evaluations, especially in tasks like translation, open-ended generation quality, and mathematical reasoning for lower-resourced languages.\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eAcross a comprehensive multilingual generative benchmark suite, Tiny Aya holds its own against leading models at this scale, advancing performance across all regions. What stands out most is the consistency Tiny Aya maintains across diverse linguistic settings. Instead of optimizing for a narrow set of headline scores, our focus was on stability, breadth, and real-world usability. We cared as much about the “how” as the “what”, meaning that we took into account factors of quality that native speakers care about, concerning fluency and coherence of a response. In practical multilingual scenarios, where efficiency, adaptability, and language coverage matter as much as raw scale, Tiny Aya is best in class and offers a compelling balance between capability and accessibility.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAccessibility as a Design Principle\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eTiny Aya is designed to run where people are: on local devices, in classrooms, in community labs, and in regions where large-scale infrastructure isn’t always available, for example translating across languages far from cloud infrastructure in a remote village. Lowering the barrier to entry means more people can build, experiment, and shape the future of multilingual AI themselves.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eBeyond the model size, accessibility also depends on how efficiently language is represented. Tiny Aya’s tokenizer was designed to reduce fragmentation across scripts and linguistic structures, producing fewer tokens per sentence across languages. This improved tokenization efficiency lowers memory and compute requirements during inference, making multilingual applications more practical on local hardware while also improving responsiveness in real-world use.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cfigure class=\"kg-card kg-image-card kg-card-hascaption\"\u003e\u003cimg src=\"https://storage.ghost.io/c/81/47/8147eb50-617d-4929-b563-3922b88421fd/content/images/2026/02/data-src-image-786f0826-f5bc-4a8f-b14a-955ab85e7735.png\" class=\"kg-image\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1490\" height=\"1076\" srcset=\"https://storage.ghost.io/c/81/47/8147eb50-617d-4929-b563-3922b88421fd/content/images/size/w600/2026/02/data-src-image-786f0826-f5bc-4a8f-b14a-955ab85e7735.png 600w, https://storage.ghost.io/c/81/47/8147eb50-617d-4929-b563-3922b88421fd/content/images/size/w1000/2026/02/data-src-image-786f0826-f5bc-4a8f-b14a-955ab85e7735.png 1000w, https://storage.ghost.io/c/81/47/8147eb50-617d-4929-b563-3922b88421fd/content/images/2026/02/data-src-image-786f0826-f5bc-4a8f-b14a-955ab85e7735.png 1490w\" sizes=\"(min-width: 720px) 720px\"\u003e\u003cfigcaption\u003e\u003ci\u003e\u003cem class=\"italic\" style=\"white-space: pre-wrap;\"\u003eTokenization efficiency across languages measured by average tokens per sequence (lower is better) when encoding the Flores dataset. Tiny Aya achieves the most efficient tokenization across the vast majority of evaluated languages, indicating improved language coverage and reduced fragmentation compared to existing multilingual tokenizers.\u003c/em\u003e\u003c/i\u003e\u003c/figcaption\u003e\u003c/figure\u003e\u003cp\u003eWe see this release as a foundation for a broader ecosystem of specialized multilingual models created by the research community itself. We invite researchers and builders everywhere to take these models and datasets and create their own language-focused systems extending coverage, exploring new evaluation strategies, and pushing multilingual AI in directions we can’t predict alone. The future of multilingual AI will not be one giant model. It will be a vibrant ecosystem of many models, shaped by many voices.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eA Family of Specialized Models\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cfigure class=\"kg-card kg-image-card kg-card-hascaption\"\u003e\u003cimg src=\"https://storage.ghost.io/c/81/47/8147eb50-617d-4929-b563-3922b88421fd/content/images/2026/02/data-src-image-b8e0588e-7cb5-406b-8e40-97f402673ef7.png\" class=\"kg-image\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1600\" height=\"900\" srcset=\"https://storage.ghost.io/c/81/47/8147eb50-617d-4929-b563-3922b88421fd/content/images/size/w600/2026/02/data-src-image-b8e0588e-7cb5-406b-8e40-97f402673ef7.png 600w, https://storage.ghost.io/c/81/47/8147eb50-617d-4929-b563-3922b88421fd/content/images/size/w1000/2026/02/data-src-image-b8e0588e-7cb5-406b-8e40-97f402673ef7.png 1000w, https://storage.ghost.io/c/81/47/8147eb50-617d-4929-b563-3922b88421fd/content/images/2026/02/data-src-image-b8e0588e-7cb5-406b-8e40-97f402673ef7.png 1600w\" sizes=\"(min-width: 720px) 720px\"\u003e\u003cfigcaption\u003e\u003ci\u003e\u003cem class=\"italic\" style=\"white-space: pre-wrap;\"\u003eTiny Aya prioritizes 67 languages in post-training from 5 regions around the globe.\u003c/em\u003e\u003c/i\u003e\u003c/figcaption\u003e\u003c/figure\u003e\u003cp\u003eTiny Aya is built on a shared multilingual foundation that enables cross-lingual learning across 70+ languages. On top of this foundation, we explored how performance can be strengthened within specific language ecosystems without sacrificing broader multilingual capability.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eTinyAya-base provides a consistent multilingual backbone across regions. The specialized variants extend this backbone by focusing more deeply on particular linguistic communities, namely Africa, South Asia, and Asia-Pacific, while retaining strong performance outside their primary region.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThis approach allows each model to develop stronger linguistic grounding and cultural nuance, creating systems that feel more natural and reliable for the communities they are meant to serve. At the same time, all Tiny Aya models retain broad multilingual coverage, making them flexible starting points for further adaptation and research.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe result is a coordinated model family of regionally specialized variants:\u003c/p\u003e\u003cul\u003e\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eTinyAya-Earth\u003c/strong\u003e: strongest for languages across Africa and West Asia regions\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eTinyAya-Fire\u003c/strong\u003e: strongest for South Asian languages\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eTinyAya-Water\u003c/strong\u003e: strongest for the Asia-Pacific and Europe region\u003c/li\u003e\u003c/ul\u003e\u003cp\u003eTogether, these models offer both a globally balanced option and region-focused variants, allowing researchers and builders to choose the right level of coverage for their use cases.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cfigure class=\"kg-card kg-image-card kg-card-hascaption\"\u003e\u003cimg src=\"https://storage.ghost.io/c/81/47/8147eb50-617d-4929-b563-3922b88421fd/content/images/2026/02/data-src-image-23e0141e-3d11-4e09-9a4f-d90826759154.png\" class=\"kg-image\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1600\" height=\"900\" srcset=\"https://storage.ghost.io/c/81/47/8147eb50-617d-4929-b563-3922b88421fd/content/images/size/w600/2026/02/data-src-image-23e0141e-3d11-4e09-9a4f-d90826759154.png 600w, https://storage.ghost.io/c/81/47/8147eb50-617d-4929-b563-3922b88421fd/content/images/size/w1000/2026/02/data-src-image-23e0141e-3d11-4e09-9a4f-d90826759154.png 1000w, https://storage.ghost.io/c/81/47/8147eb50-617d-4929-b563-3922b88421fd/content/images/2026/02/data-src-image-23e0141e-3d11-4e09-9a4f-d90826759154.png 1600w\" sizes=\"(min-width: 720px) 720px\"\u003e\u003cfigcaption\u003e\u003ci\u003e\u003cem class=\"italic\" style=\"white-space: pre-wrap;\"\u003eWe release four specialized models each with a unique strength profile across regions.\u0026nbsp;\u003c/em\u003e\u003c/i\u003e\u003c/figcaption\u003e\u003c/figure\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eTry Tiny Aya Now\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eTiny Aya is available today as open weight models. You can explore and use Tiny Aya family of models in multiple ways:\u003c/p\u003e\u003cul\u003e\u003cli\u003eTry it instantly on \u003ca href=\"https://huggingface.co/spaces/CohereLabs/tiny-aya\"\u003e\u003cu\u003e\u003cem\u003eHugging Face Spac\u003c/em\u003ee\u003c/u\u003e\u003c/a\u003e or on the \u003cem\u003eCohere platform\u003c/em\u003e.\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003eDownload the weights on \u003ca href=\"https://huggingface.co/collections/CohereLabs/tiny-aya\"\u003e\u003cem\u003e\u003cu\u003eHugging Face\u003c/u\u003e\u003c/em\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\u003cem\u003e and \u003c/em\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://www.kaggle.com/models/cohere-labs/tiny-aya/\"\u003e\u003cem\u003e\u003cu\u003eKaggle\u003c/u\u003e\u003c/em\u003e\u003c/a\u003e for local deployment and research use.\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003eLearn more in our \u003ca href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.11510\" rel=\"noreferrer\"\u003e\u003cu\u003edetailed technical report \u003c/u\u003e\u003c/a\u003edescribing the training strategy, comprehensive evaluations, and design decisions behind Tiny Aya.\u0026nbsp;\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003eReady to build with Tiny Aya? Learn more about \u003ca href=\"https://www.notion.so/cohereai/Expedition-Tiny-Aya-2f04398375db804c93c4c9f5fbb94833?source=copy_link\" rel=\"noreferrer\"\u003eExpedition Tiny Aya\u003c/a\u003e- a multi-phase, mentor-supported research challenge designed to catalyze new projects using the Tiny Aya model family\u003c/li\u003e\u003c/ul\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cem\u003e*TinyAya-Base covers 70+ languages including: Amharic, Arabic, Basque, Bengali, Bulgarian, Burmese, Cantonese, Catalan, Chinese (simplified and traditional), Croatian, Czech, Danish, Dutch, English, Estonian, Finnish, French, Galician, German, Greek, Gujarati, Hausa, Hebrew, Hindi, Hungarian, Igbo, Indonesian, Irish, Italian, Japanese, Javanese, Khmer, Korean, Lao, Latvian, Lithuanian, Malagasy, Malay, Maltese, Marathi, Nepali, Nigerian Pidgin, Norwegian (Bokmål), Persian, Polish, Portuguese, Punjabi, Romanian, Russian, Serbian, Shona, Slovak, Slovenian, Spanish, Swahili, Swedish, Tagalog, Tamil, Telugu, Thai, Turkish, Ukrainian, Urdu, Vietnamese, Welsh, Wolof, Xhosa, Yoruba and Zulu.\u003c/em\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003chr\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cem\u003eTiny Aya: Bridging Scale and Multilingual Depth\u003c/em\u003e (2026), by Alejandro R. Salamanca, Diana Abagyan, Daniel D’souza, Ammar Khairi, David Mora, Saurabh Dash, Viraat Aryabumi, Sara Rajaee, Mehrnaz Mofakhami, Ananya Sahu, Thomas Euyang, Brittawnya Prince, Madeline Smith, Hangyu Lin, Acyr Locatelli, Sara Hooker, Tom Kocmi, Aidan Gomez, Ivan Zhang, Phil Blunsom, Nick Frosst, Joelle Pineau, Beyza Ermis, Ahmet Üstün, Julia Kreutzer, Marzieh Fadaee. \u003ca href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.11510\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"\u003ehttps://arxiv.org/abs/2603.11510\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e34:T53b,For embedding, request profiles can vary dramatically. Think about an e-commerce search application for a moment. Before a shopper can search for anything, every product in the store has to be turned into something the search system can match against. So the application takes each product description, runs it through the embedding model, and stores the result. That's the indexing job, a big one the first time through and then a steady stream of smaller batches as inventory changes.To get through it efficiently, the application sends products in bulk: say, around 100 descriptions in a single request, each roughly 150 words (~200 tokens). The user is not waiting on instant results, so what matters is finishing the job, not finishing it fast.\n\nNow contrast that with a shopper typing \"waterproof trail running shoes\" into the search box. Same model, but this time it's one short query and the user is watching a loading spinner until it comes back.\n\nBoth scenarios involve embedding workloads, but they optimize for opposite things. Batch processing is about throughput and price-performance - getting the most work done per dollar. While the single query is about responsiveness, and you accept lower price-performance to achieve it.\n\nFor the batch processing and search query scenarios, an embeddings profile might look like this:35:T55d,Rerank workloads introduce another crucial variable into the mix: how many candidate documents you're actually asking the model to evaluate.\n\nLet's stick with our product search example. First, your vector search might pull up 50 candidate products that match the query. Then the reranker steps in, it's like having a knowledgeable sales associate who looks at those 50 options and decides which ones truly deserve the top spots.\n\nThe model essentially compares the shopper's query against each candidate and ranks them by relevance. Each query-document pair is scored independently, which means the query gets processed once per candidate - so the work scales directly with how many candidates you send.\n\nA typical rerank request looks like this: one short query of around 20 tokens, plus 50 candidate products at roughly 200 tokens each. That's about 11,000 tokens the model works through for a single search.\n\nSimple enough, right? But here's where things get interesting. Suppose you change your strategy from reranking 20 candidates to 50 or 100. On the surface nothing changes for the user- same number of searches, same experience. Behind the scenes you've gone from about 4,400 tokens per search to about 22,000. It's the difference between asking someone to pick the best 20 products from a catalog versus ranking 100 of them. Same task, far more to read through.\n36:T47b,The first three rows are the same model at the same price. The only thing that changes is the shape of the request, and the crossover moves from around four requests per minute to tens of thousands. A short query has to arrive in enormous volume before dedicated capacity is worth it. A batch of long documents gets there almost immediately.\n\nThat has a practical implication for the catalog workload described earlier. At 30 requests per minute it sounds like a small job, but the break-even for that exact request profile is ~20 requests per minute. It is already in the range where dedicated capacity is the more economical choice, despite the modest request rate.\n\nComparing across models needs more care. Embedding is billed per token and reranking per search unit, and their dedicated capacity costs differ, so ~20 and ~29 requests per minute aren't two points on the same scale. Reranking's crossover landing between the catalog and query profiles is a coincidence of these particular request shapes, not a property of the models.\n\nChange the compute capacity, the pricing model, or the utilization pattern and the break even point changes.37:Te8c,\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cem\u003eNew space deepens Cohere’s commitment to Canada’s capital and growing AI ecosystem\u003c/em\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eToday, we’re announcing Cohere’s first office in Ottawa, expanding our presence in Canada and establishing a new home for our growing team in the nation’s capital.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eCohere was founded in Canada, and we continue to believe deeply in the country’s role in the future of artificial intelligence. Ottawa’s deep technical talent, strong research community, and long history of technology innovation make it a natural place for Cohere to grow.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eAdjacent to Lansdowne Park in one of Ottawa’s most vibrant and well-connected urban districts, the new office will give Cohere employees a dedicated space to collaborate and meet with customers and partners. Cohere currently has nearly 30 employees across the Ottawa region, spanning go-to-market, engineering, infrastructure, security, and sovereign AI functions, with that presence expected to expand as the city plays a growing role in our Canadian operations.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cem\u003e“Canada is home for Cohere. It’s where we founded the company, where so much of our talent comes from, and where we want to keep building for the long term. Ottawa has an incredible technology community and a deep pool of technical talent. Establishing our first office in the city is an important step in investing in our team and building for the future.”\u003c/em\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cem\u003eAidan Gomez\u003c/em\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cem\u003eCo-Founder and CEO\u003c/em\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cem\u003e\"Ottawa is key to Canada’s AI ecosystem, connecting Canadian AI innovators with key decision-makers and policy experts, namely within the Government of Canada. Cohere's decision to open a new office here in our nation's capital will reaffirm Ottawa's role as a point of collaboration, spurring further investment, opportunity and innovation in our community and across Canada.\"\u0026nbsp;\u003c/em\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cem\u003eYasir Naqvi M.P. \u003c/em\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cem\u003eMember of Parliament for Ottawa Centre\u0026nbsp;\u003c/em\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eA New Home in Ottawa\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eOttawa has a long history as a technology hub, with strengths spanning telecommunications, cybersecurity, infrastructure, and advanced computing. Its leading research institutions, growing AI community, and unique concentration of public and private sector organizations make the city an important part of Canada’s technology landscape.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eFor Cohere, establishing a home in Ottawa will create more opportunities to engage with the businesses, institutions, and public sector organizations adopting AI, while deepening our connections across the city’s technology ecosystem.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eEarlier this year, we announced a naming-rights partnership that saw the city’s largest purpose-built convention and event facility become the \u003ca href=\"https://coherecentre.ca/news/\"\u003e\u003cu\u003eCohere Centre\u003c/u\u003e\u003c/a\u003e. The partnership reflects our long-term investment in Ottawa as a centre for technology, business, and innovation.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eOur new office builds on that momentum by giving our local team a permanent home and creating more opportunities to connect with the people and organizations shaping the region.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eGrowing in Canada\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eOur investment in Ottawa is part of Cohere’s broader commitment to building, deploying, and commercializing advanced AI from Canada while serving customers around the world.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eAs we grow, we remain focused on investing in Canadian talent, strengthening the country’s AI ecosystem, and helping organizations adopt secure, sovereign AI.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eCanada helped shape the modern AI era. We’re proud to keep building here, with Ottawa playing an increasingly important role in Cohere’s future.\u003c/p\u003e6:[\"$\",\"$L2d\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$L2e\",null,{\"darkMode\":false}],[\"$\",\"$L2f\",null,{\"_createdAt\":\"2026-10-01T14:40:41Z\",\"_id\":\"b2ed5943-bf59-4913-a146-cb76bb8935d0\",\"_rev\":\"namk7zdkPF3v8Gb9lHa64h\",\"_type\":\"announcementBanner\",\"_updatedAt\":\"2026-10-05T12:58:11Z\",\"bannerColor\":{\"label\":\"black\",\"value\":\"#000000\"},\"bannerIcon\":{\"isHighFetchPriority\":false},\"bannerLabel\":\"Introducing North 2: AI without compromises\",\"language\":\"en-US\",\"link\":{\"isExternal\":true,\"label\":\"Learn more about the updates\",\"url\":\"/blog/introducing-north-2\"},\"translatedUsingAI\":false}],\"$undefined\",[\"$\",\"$L27\",null,{\"title\":\"Cohere\",\"navItems\":\"$22:props:children:0:props:navItems\",\"primaryCtaLink\":\"$22:props:children:0:props:primaryCtaLink\",\"ctaButton\":\"$22:props:children:0:props:ctaButton\",\"bannerEnabled\":true,\"navTheme\":\"lightTransparent\",\"isSimpleNav\":\"$undefined\",\"useCustomNav\":\"$undefined\",\"customNav\":\"$undefined\",\"locales\":\"$5:props:value:localesConfig:locales\",\"navbarFeaturedBlogPosts\":\"$22:props:children:0:props:navbarFeaturedBlogPosts\"}],[\"$\",\"$L30\",null,{\"backgroundImageClasses\":\"relative z-background !bg-[url('/base-page/base-page-bg.webp')]\",\"background\":\"$undefined\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$L31\",null,{\"url\":\"https://cohere.com/blog/cohere-labs-tiny-aya\",\"headline\":\"Cohere Labs Launches Tiny Aya for Multilingual AI | Cohere\",\"image\":\"https://cohere-ai.ghost.io/content/images/size/w1200/2026/02/TinyAya_BlogHeader.png\",\"datePublished\":\"2026-02-17T04:00:07.000-05:00\",\"dateModified\":\"2026-05-01T10:42:59.000-04:00\",\"author\":[{\"name\":\"Cohere Labs Team\",\"url\":\"/blog/authors/Cohere Labs Team\"}],\"description\":\"Cohere Labs launches Tiny Aya, a compact open multilingual model that brings capable AI to 70+ languages on modest hardware.\",\"publisherName\":\"Cohere\",\"publisherLogo\":\"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/rjtqmwfu/web3-prod/5b7ba1e2dedf61f5bc9285a0cce50b8e8cda9a72-118x20.svg\",\"faqItems\":[]}],[\"$\",\"$L32\",null,{\"ghostBlogData\":{\"slug\":\"cohere-labs-tiny-aya\",\"id\":\"698f8dcfc64c5e0001ed04db\",\"uuid\":\"20b75c11-8d4e-46e3-82db-f6abbdd695ee\",\"title\":\"Cohere Labs Launches Tiny Aya, Making Multilingual AI Accessible\",\"html\":\"$33\",\"comment_id\":\"698f8dcfc64c5e0001ed04db\",\"feature_image\":\"https://storage.ghost.io/c/81/47/8147eb50-617d-4929-b563-3922b88421fd/content/images/2026/02/TinyAya_BlogHeader.png\",\"featured\":false,\"visibility\":\"public\",\"created_at\":\"2026-02-13T15:47:11.000-05:00\",\"updated_at\":\"2026-05-01T10:42:59.000-04:00\",\"published_at\":\"2026-02-17T04:00:07.000-05:00\",\"custom_excerpt\":null,\"codeinjection_head\":null,\"codeinjection_foot\":null,\"custom_template\":null,\"canonical_url\":null,\"tags\":[{\"id\":\"638935288561ce003d350ce9\",\"name\":\"Research\",\"slug\":\"research\",\"description\":null,\"feature_image\":null,\"visibility\":\"public\",\"og_image\":null,\"og_title\":null,\"og_description\":null,\"twitter_image\":null,\"twitter_title\":null,\"twitter_description\":null,\"meta_title\":null,\"meta_description\":null,\"codeinjection_head\":null,\"codeinjection_foot\":null,\"canonical_url\":null,\"accent_color\":null,\"url\":\"https://cohere-ai.ghost.io/tag/research/\"}],\"authors\":[{\"id\":\"639b460e676837003ded3375\",\"name\":\"Cohere Labs Team\",\"slug\":\"cohere-labs-team\",\"profile_image\":\"https://storage.ghost.io/c/81/47/8147eb50-617d-4929-b563-3922b88421fd/content/images/2022/12/PavisyFb_400x400.png\",\"cover_image\":null,\"bio\":null,\"website\":\"http://cohere.com/research\",\"location\":null,\"facebook\":null,\"twitter\":\"@Cohere_Labs\",\"meta_title\":null,\"meta_description\":null,\"threads\":null,\"bluesky\":null,\"mastodon\":null,\"tiktok\":null,\"youtube\":null,\"instagram\":null,\"linkedin\":null,\"url\":\"https://cohere-ai.ghost.io/author/cohere-labs-team/\"}],\"primary_author\":{\"id\":\"639b460e676837003ded3375\",\"name\":\"Cohere Labs Team\",\"slug\":\"cohere-labs-team\",\"profile_image\":\"https://storage.ghost.io/c/81/47/8147eb50-617d-4929-b563-3922b88421fd/content/images/2022/12/PavisyFb_400x400.png\",\"cover_image\":null,\"bio\":null,\"website\":\"http://cohere.com/research\",\"location\":null,\"facebook\":null,\"twitter\":\"@Cohere_Labs\",\"meta_title\":null,\"meta_description\":null,\"threads\":null,\"bluesky\":null,\"mastodon\":null,\"tiktok\":null,\"youtube\":null,\"instagram\":null,\"linkedin\":null,\"url\":\"https://cohere-ai.ghost.io/author/cohere-labs-team/\"},\"primary_tag\":{\"id\":\"638935288561ce003d350ce9\",\"name\":\"Research\",\"slug\":\"research\",\"description\":null,\"feature_image\":null,\"visibility\":\"public\",\"og_image\":null,\"og_title\":null,\"og_description\":null,\"twitter_image\":null,\"twitter_title\":null,\"twitter_description\":null,\"meta_title\":null,\"meta_description\":null,\"codeinjection_head\":null,\"codeinjection_foot\":null,\"canonical_url\":null,\"accent_color\":null,\"url\":\"https://cohere-ai.ghost.io/tag/research/\"},\"url\":\"https://cohere-ai.ghost.io/cohere-labs-tiny-aya/\",\"excerpt\":\"Open-weight AI designed for real-world languages that is powerful, adaptable, and efficient enough to run locally, even on a phone.\\n\\nToday, Cohere Labs, Cohere’s research arm, is introducing Tiny Aya, the most capable multilingual open-weight model at its scale. Tiny Aya delivers state-of-the-art translation quality, strong multilingual understanding, top-quality target language responses, and broad language coverage in a model small enough to run locally, even on consumer hardware and mobile ph\",\"reading_time\":7,\"access\":true,\"comments\":false,\"og_image\":null,\"og_title\":null,\"og_description\":null,\"twitter_image\":null,\"twitter_title\":null,\"twitter_description\":null,\"meta_title\":\"Cohere Labs Launches Tiny Aya for Multilingual AI | Cohere\",\"meta_description\":\"Cohere Labs launches Tiny Aya, a compact open multilingual model that brings capable AI to 70+ languages on modest hardware.\",\"email_subject\":null,\"frontmatter\":null,\"feature_image_alt\":null,\"feature_image_caption\":null},\"latestPosts\":[{\"_id\":\"0765becc-0e99-41e1-9fb4-b0ba006d3e04\",\"authorReferences\":[{\"_createdAt\":\"2026-03-20T18:40:41Z\",\"_id\":\"3W0lOxv818bkXUoUKMH8vt\",\"_rev\":\"9CTItyT3Ypx2CnWeTdXY9I\",\"_type\":\"blogAuthor\",\"_updatedAt\":\"2026-06-15T20:07:29Z\",\"bio\":\"Solutions Architect, Partnerships\",\"fullName\":\"Payal Singh\",\"ghostSlug\":\"payal\",\"language\":\"en-US\",\"profileImage\":{\"_type\":\"object\",\"altText\":\"Portrait of Payal Singh\",\"file\":{\"_type\":\"image\",\"asset\":{\"_ref\":\"image-a1a1526fec98280ac28c768ba9cdd69f34d5143d-200x200-jpg\",\"_type\":\"reference\"}}},\"slug\":{\"_type\":\"slug\",\"current\":\"blog/authors/payal-singh\"}}],\"body\":[{\"_key\":\"06535cfe500f\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"773aa7af9bba\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"strong\"],\"text\":\"Shared or Dedicated Inference for Embed and Rerank? Start With the Workload\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"h5\"},{\"_key\":\"59de57a8ce6c\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"838338f6952c\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"Choosing between shared, consumption-based inference and dedicated, provisioned inference is not simply a question of which option has the lower price.\\n\\nFor embedding and reranking workloads, the answer depends heavily on how the application uses the model. A useful way to think about the decision is:\\n\\n\"},{\"_key\":\"acaba8ca1acc\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"strong\",\"em\"],\"text\":\"Request Profile - \u003e Traffic Pattern - \u003e Utilization - \u003e Deployment Choice\\n\\n\"},{\"_key\":\"f90ae0ae57b9\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"Understanding these factors can tell you much more than comparing headline prices.\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"2d2fe67c2052\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"0f536337b014\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"strong\"],\"text\":\"Start with the request profile\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"h5\"},{\"_key\":\"3a5d84088325\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"4e505a2c6ab4\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"The first question is: \"},{\"_key\":\"9c0cb878db7c\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"em\"],\"text\":\"What does a typical model request actually look like?\\n\\n\"},{\"_key\":\"7b5a157be3ed\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"$34\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"ecefa8442366\",\"_type\":\"image\",\"asset\":{\"_ref\":\"image-6daa4331a9fa7f67be3dc70b81a5f1f0af18842b-1441x834-png\",\"_type\":\"reference\"},\"horizontalAlignment\":\"items-center\",\"size\":\"md\"},{\"_key\":\"b22af14d4c83\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"a792f7d5f49b\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"You're not running the same workload twice, you're asking one model to do two very different jobs. Infrastructure tuned for large batches won't necessarily deliver the same response times when serving thousands of small, latency-sensitive requests.\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"7a24e6a73f05\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"760a732f1d6a\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"strong\"],\"text\":\"Reranking has a different request shape\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"h5\"},{\"_key\":\"7c2823154b82\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"b08fb49841be\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"$35\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"cb0a192e1ac7\",\"_type\":\"image\",\"asset\":{\"_ref\":\"image-d5e278ed9d5abe9ed70998ffd9165f07b747b574-1419x729-png\",\"_type\":\"reference\"},\"horizontalAlignment\":\"items-center\",\"size\":\"md\"},{\"_key\":\"54059f93f747\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"3726b2e05dd4\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"This is why retrieval depth becomes such a critical infrastructure decision. You're not just scaling based on user volume anymore, you're scaling based on how deep you want to dig into your results pool.\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"2572aa9de533\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"0cc00af44a7a\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"strong\"],\"text\":\"Traffic pattern matters as much as traffic volume\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"h5\"},{\"_key\":\"3bca943d8086\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"3405c77e4cb9\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"Once the request profile is understood, the next question is: \"},{\"_key\":\"927bc5c0580e\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"em\"],\"text\":\"How does traffic arrive?\\n\\n\"},{\"_key\":\"e1c5dd1d3827\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"Two applications can have the same peak traffic and still have very different infrastructure economics.\\n\\n\"},{\"_key\":\"072cc89705fe\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"strong\"],\"text\":\"Sustained Traffic Patterns\\n\\n\"},{\"_key\":\"af31943f6640\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"Think about a global search service, traffic flows at a relatively consistent pace throughout the day. There's no drastic surge, no dramatic dips. In this scenario, dedicated capacity starts looking really attractive because your infrastructure stays nicely utilized. You're not paying for capacity that sits idle most of the time, and you're never scrambling to handle unexpected spikes.\\n\\n\"},{\"_key\":\"81d8384ec4e5\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"strong\"],\"text\":\"Bursty Traffic Patterns\\n\\n\"},{\"_key\":\"525d4213a7b7\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"Now picture a different kind of application, maybe a financial reporting system or a batch processing job. This application maintains low traffic volume during standard operational periods, with concentrated spikes occurring during peak business hours or scheduled processing windows. For this scenario, consumption-based services are a good option because you're not paying for all that idle capacity during those low traffic volumes.\\n\\nThe real infrastructure question\\n\\nIt's not simply \"},{\"_key\":\"21960a5215dd\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"em\"],\"text\":\"\\\"How high is peak traffic?\\\"\\n\\n\"},{\"_key\":\"16687eb63b28\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"It's \"},{\"_key\":\"8289c985517d\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"em\"],\"text\":\"\\\"How consistently can the infrastructure be utilized?\\\"\\n\\n\"},{\"_key\":\"dd44e0029ced\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"Peak traffic tells you your maximum capacity needs, but utilization patterns tell you how efficiently you'll actually use that capacity. A service that handles 10,000 requests per second but only for 5 minutes a day has very different economics than one that handles 100 requests per second constantly. Getting this right means the difference between infrastructure that's just adequate and infrastructure that's truly optimal with respect to being cost-effective and responsive.\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"a821e07b6a0c\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"e199391697a6\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"strong\"],\"text\":\"Beyond request volume: shape and pattern matter more\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"h5\"},{\"_key\":\"d90953fc564f\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"55b1786e47d3\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"Looking only at request volume leads to poor infrastructure decisions. Consider these contrasting scenarios:\\n\\n\"},{\"_key\":\"6e6891de4a3a\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"em\"],\"text\":\"Low RPM, High Processing\"},{\"_key\":\"48b45ad8d608\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\": A catalog embedding workload with just 30 requests per minute might seem small, but if each request contains 100 texts × 200 tokens, that's 600K tokens per minute of sustained processing. Dedicated capacity may be well-utilized.\\n\\n\"},{\"_key\":\"61b59102a115\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"em\"],\"text\":\"High RPM, Low Processing\"},{\"_key\":\"f45a7f8455e4\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\": Thousands of query embedding requests per minute sounds demanding, but if each contains only a few tokens and traffic spikes last just hours, shared infrastructure may be more cost-effective.\\n\\n\"},{\"_key\":\"80b4268f1f7c\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"em\"],\"text\":\"Reranking follows the same pattern\"},{\"_key\":\"e14ed9a832e2\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\": Few searches with large candidate sets can outweigh many searches with small sets.\\n\\nKey distinction:\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"e84e22c999d1\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"58a0f900667d\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"Request rate tells you \"},{\"_key\":\"9854d9fc2f88\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"em\"],\"text\":\"how often\"},{\"_key\":\"954afc4894be\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\" the model is called\"}],\"level\":1,\"listItem\":\"bullet\",\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"de0b1c356120\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"be85f34aba37\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"Request shape tells you \"},{\"_key\":\"3701c156143b\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"em\"],\"text\":\"how much work\"},{\"_key\":\"6bb2ec3133dd\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\" each call represents\"}],\"level\":1,\"listItem\":\"bullet\",\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"a786bd10f9a5\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"f43107f6d99a\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"Traffic patterns tell you \"},{\"_key\":\"91c679caa979\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"em\"],\"text\":\"how consistently\"},{\"_key\":\"a20ad5ce75b3\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\" capacity is needed\"}],\"level\":1,\"listItem\":\"bullet\",\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"6e97afae1858\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"6352afb0744d\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"strong\"],\"text\":\"The cost crossover depends on the workload\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"h5\"},{\"_key\":\"5abac7db2958\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"a799cf937d1f\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"Once request shape and traffic pattern are understood, you can estimate the point at which dedicated infrastructure becomes more economical than consumption-based inference. There is no universal break-even point, the crossover is specific to the workload.\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"f6ed1ad06aba\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"a0490d317f84\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"em\"],\"text\":\"For example, using the representative workloads and assumptions in this analysis:\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"18741b277aa0\",\"_type\":\"tableBlock\",\"rows\":[{\"_key\":\"fe9b6db9-ec21-43b3-abdc-e19517cb6e6f\",\"_type\":\"tableRow\",\"cells\":[\"Workload\",\"Request Profile\",\"Processing per request\",\"Calculated break-even\"]},{\"_key\":\"63a8a5cf-2276-4552-bdd1-9c9bda60cc04\",\"_type\":\"tableRow\",\"cells\":[\"Query Embedding\",\"1 Short query\",\"~8 tokens\",\"tens of thousands of req/min\"]},{\"_key\":\"c85dabdc-48e4-46b6-9995-f2bb845c8fa7\",\"_type\":\"tableRow\",\"cells\":[\"Catalog Embedding\",\"100 texts * 200 tokens\",\"~20K tokens\",\"~20 req/min\"]},{\"_key\":\"21775f70-758a-4ddd-996b-5cb62dda11d9\",\"_type\":\"tableRow\",\"cells\":[\"Long-document embedding\",\"100 texts *1000 tokens\",\"~100K tokens\",\"~4 req/min\"]},{\"_key\":\"021ebd9d-301a-48c5-8301-c0a6d8b84205\",\"_type\":\"tableRow\",\"cells\":[\"Reranking\",\"1 query + 50 docs (20 query tokens, 200/doc)\",\"~11K tokens\",\"~29 req/min\"]}]},{\"_key\":\"1017b42af7ac\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"9f48135effef\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"em\"],\"text\":\"**\"},{\"_key\":\"8a91c133ea38\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"em\",\"sub\"],\"text\":\"These break-evens are based on representative consumption-based pricing and a single dedicated NVIDIA A10 GPU instance billed hourly, assuming no negotiated discounts and traffic sustained 24×7. Workloads that run only part of the day require a higher request rate during active hours to reach the same economic crossover. A different instance type changes both cost and capacity, potentially shifting the break-even points. Instance throughput determines how much volume one instance can handle before additional capacity is required; it does not directly determine the economic crossover, which is based on pricing. Embedding is billed per token and reranking per search unit, so their request rates are not directly comparable. These figures are illustrative, workload-specific crossovers, not universal deployment thresholds.\\n\\n\"},{\"_key\":\"80a3f36dfc43\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"$36\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"7bc7f91ec609\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"2f5e4c49bc25\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"strong\"],\"text\":\"Latency changes the equation\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"h5\"},{\"_key\":\"47d354e1f0d4\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"f75be73b3b19\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"Maximum throughput doesn't equal usable capacity because different workloads have fundamentally different performance requirements.\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"1aaaee726d89\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"11faa6966276\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"Batch indexing: Can operate at peak throughput since completion efficiency matters more than immediate response\"}],\"level\":1,\"listItem\":\"bullet\",\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"904c0b0440f0\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"8dc0387a39aa\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"Search queries: Require strict latency targets - response time directly impacts user experience and business outcomes\"}],\"level\":1,\"listItem\":\"bullet\",\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"cc2496cd7d3c\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"b47565c10223\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"Reranking: Expanding candidate sets improves documents/sec processed while lowering the searches/sec served\"}],\"level\":1,\"listItem\":\"bullet\",\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"924a5113bec1\",\"_type\":\"image\",\"asset\":{\"_ref\":\"image-413c88f767178f4ef398d764ec282289fe68b5b8-1551x815-png\",\"_type\":\"reference\"},\"horizontalAlignment\":\"items-center\",\"size\":\"md\"},{\"_key\":\"3f404692d74b\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"24eb6a083b07\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"This latency-throughput trade-off means infrastructure sizing must account for both performance dimensions. For interactive workloads, capacity should be based on sustainable throughput that meets target latency requirements, not just maximum benchmark performance. This often necessitates additional headroom beyond peak demand calculations.\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"7f4406ccf383\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"af23b2235936\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"strong\"],\"text\":\"One simple decision framework\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"h5\"},{\"_key\":\"97a178e7f8e2\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"e9c9f6428622\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"Before choosing shared or dedicated inference for Embed or Rerank, answer these questions and the infrastructure decision becomes much easier\\n\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"f2800df71ba0\",\"_type\":\"tableBlock\",\"rows\":[{\"_key\":\"b2b70eab-23d5-4fac-9cc6-4828452049ca\",\"_type\":\"tableRow\",\"cells\":[\"Question\",\"Why it Matters\"]},{\"_key\":\"7d7fca8f-9237-40c5-8c62-af9084f5542c\",\"_type\":\"tableRow\",\"cells\":[\"What does a typical request contain?\",\"Defines the amount of work per request\"]},{\"_key\":\"33811279-c511-470a-bcd9-b3cabc3b8031\",\"_type\":\"tableRow\",\"cells\":[\"Is this document or query embedding?\",\"Throughput and latency requirements differ\"]},{\"_key\":\"bf17b265-19cb-470f-bc94-84f738c7eea5\",\"_type\":\"tableRow\",\"cells\":[\"How many documents are reranked?\",\"Directly affects rerank compute\"]},{\"_key\":\"a12039ea-4426-4bb1-804f-f21e7f283ff6\",\"_type\":\"tableRow\",\"cells\":[\"What is the expected request rate?\",\"Determines infrastructure demand\"]},{\"_key\":\"95f3d1a2-ec47-41ac-91b0-4c854a639901\",\"_type\":\"tableRow\",\"cells\":[\"Is traffic steady or bursty?\",\"Determines achievable utilization\"]},{\"_key\":\"16410776-bce2-487d-b8fd-6b748b5cf204\",\"_type\":\"tableRow\",\"cells\":[\"What latency does the application require?\",\"Determines usable capacity\"]},{\"_key\":\"e90f7f64-2aa3-49f0-9ab9-e2df72bf5047\",\"_type\":\"tableRow\",\"cells\":[\"Will dedicated infrastructure stay busy?\",\"Determines whether its economics are attractive\"]}]},{\"_key\":\"cba0e8882fa4\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"b045f80c1ef2\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"strong\"],\"text\":\"Takeaway\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"h5\"},{\"_key\":\"d7e2c9bdc5da\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"031cc22ea061\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"When people start comparing shared and dedicated inference, they usually reach for two numbers first, the price per token and the requests per minute. Neither one really tells you much on its own. What you actually want to know is how much work each request is carrying, how steadily it shows up, and whether your capacity is going to stay busy.\\n\\nSo there's no magic break-even number to look up. The crossover belongs to your workload, not to the deployment option you picked. And many search applications end up using both, dedicated capacity for the catalog indexing and consumption pricing for the query traffic that spikes based on end user demand.\\n\\nUnderstand the request → Understand the traffic → Understand the utilization → Then pick the deployment model.\"},{\"_key\":\"b045f80c1ef2\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"strong\"],\"text\":\"\\n\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"}],\"date\":\"2026-10-09T18:54:00.000Z\",\"heroImage\":{\"isHighFetchPriority\":false},\"slug\":{\"_type\":\"slug\",\"current\":\"blog/shared-or-dedicated-inference-for-embed-rerank\"},\"tagReferences\":[{\"_createdAt\":\"2026-04-02T17:16:20Z\",\"_id\":\"0eb03815-99e5-4650-a83d-3b68615463ea\",\"_rev\":\"jtzXmr5sPTxAjhSjhwzZPz\",\"_system\":{\"base\":{\"id\":\"0eb03815-99e5-4650-a83d-3b68615463ea\",\"rev\":\"usPbImjoC2LtIOcJmuwnms\"}},\"_type\":\"blogTag\",\"_updatedAt\":\"2026-10-07T20:01:43Z\",\"emergingTopic\":true,\"language\":\"en-US\",\"slug\":{\"_type\":\"slug\",\"current\":\"blog/tag/enterprise-ai\"},\"tag\":[\"Enterprise AI\"]},{\"_createdAt\":\"2026-03-17T21:41:28Z\",\"_id\":\"2db47c34-69d9-44a4-a405-a350b5fa7d78\",\"_rev\":\"wVWqNP6lLdMYvKSzOkkhhK\",\"_system\":{\"base\":{\"id\":\"2db47c34-69d9-44a4-a405-a350b5fa7d78\",\"rev\":\"hJd2TWJcSRp2kKD4aM5VuE\"}},\"_type\":\"blogTag\",\"_updatedAt\":\"2026-10-07T20:01:24Z\",\"language\":\"en-US\",\"slug\":{\"_type\":\"slug\",\"current\":\"blog/tag/technology\"},\"tag\":[\"Technology\"]}],\"title\":\"Shared or dedicated inference for Embed \u0026 Rerank\",\"url\":\"/blog/shared-or-dedicated-inference-for-embed-rerank\"},{\"id\":\"6ac790ce8308b40001b0661c\",\"uuid\":\"d45bff63-cfa7-43c3-b05d-eca1758ff04b\",\"title\":\"Cohere Expands Canadian Footprint with New Ottawa Office\",\"slug\":\"cohere-expands-canadian-footprint-with-new-ottawa-office\",\"html\":\"$37\",\"comment_id\":\"6ac790ce8308b40001b0661c\",\"feature_image\":\"https://storage.ghost.io/c/81/47/8147eb50-617d-4929-b563-3922b88421fd/content/images/2026/10/260902_OfficeAnnouncement_Ottawa_Blog.png\",\"featured\":false,\"visibility\":\"public\",\"created_at\":\"2026-10-08T08:47:10.000-04:00\",\"updated_at\":\"2026-10-08T08:53:33.000-04:00\",\"published_at\":\"2026-10-08T08:50:00.000-04:00\",\"custom_excerpt\":null,\"codeinjection_head\":null,\"codeinjection_foot\":null,\"custom_template\":null,\"canonical_url\":null,\"tags\":[{\"id\":\"6a188c7d7582b300013552d9\",\"name\":\"Company News\",\"slug\":\"company-news\",\"description\":null,\"feature_image\":null,\"visibility\":\"public\",\"og_image\":null,\"og_title\":null,\"og_description\":null,\"twitter_image\":null,\"twitter_title\":null,\"twitter_description\":null,\"meta_title\":null,\"meta_description\":null,\"codeinjection_head\":null,\"codeinjection_foot\":null,\"canonical_url\":null,\"accent_color\":null,\"url\":\"https://cohere-ai.ghost.io/tag/company-news/\"}],\"authors\":[{\"id\":\"62731e5fd72e3b003da48586\",\"name\":\"Cohere Team\",\"slug\":\"cohere\",\"profile_image\":\"https://storage.ghost.io/c/81/47/8147eb50-617d-4929-b563-3922b88421fd/content/images/2023/03/Cohere-Symbol-Color-RGB.png\",\"cover_image\":null,\"bio\":null,\"website\":null,\"location\":null,\"facebook\":null,\"twitter\":null,\"meta_title\":null,\"meta_description\":null,\"threads\":null,\"bluesky\":null,\"mastodon\":null,\"tiktok\":null,\"youtube\":null,\"instagram\":null,\"linkedin\":null,\"url\":\"https://cohere-ai.ghost.io/author/cohere/\"}],\"primary_author\":{\"id\":\"62731e5fd72e3b003da48586\",\"name\":\"Cohere Team\",\"slug\":\"cohere\",\"profile_image\":\"https://storage.ghost.io/c/81/47/8147eb50-617d-4929-b563-3922b88421fd/content/images/2023/03/Cohere-Symbol-Color-RGB.png\",\"cover_image\":null,\"bio\":null,\"website\":null,\"location\":null,\"facebook\":null,\"twitter\":null,\"meta_title\":null,\"meta_description\":null,\"threads\":null,\"bluesky\":null,\"mastodon\":null,\"tiktok\":null,\"youtube\":null,\"instagram\":null,\"linkedin\":null,\"url\":\"https://cohere-ai.ghost.io/author/cohere/\"},\"primary_tag\":{\"id\":\"6a188c7d7582b300013552d9\",\"name\":\"Company News\",\"slug\":\"company-news\",\"description\":null,\"feature_image\":null,\"visibility\":\"public\",\"og_image\":null,\"og_title\":null,\"og_description\":null,\"twitter_image\":null,\"twitter_title\":null,\"twitter_description\":null,\"meta_title\":null,\"meta_description\":null,\"codeinjection_head\":null,\"codeinjection_foot\":null,\"canonical_url\":null,\"accent_color\":null,\"url\":\"https://cohere-ai.ghost.io/tag/company-news/\"},\"url\":\"/blog/cohere-expands-canadian-footprint-with-new-ottawa-office\",\"excerpt\":\"New space deepens Cohere’s commitment to Canada’s capital and growing AI ecosystem\\n\\nToday, we’re announcing Cohere’s first office in Ottawa, expanding our presence in Canada and establishing a new home for our growing team in the nation’s capital.\\n\\nCohere was founded in Canada, and we continue to believe deeply in the country’s role in the future of artificial intelligence. Ottawa’s deep technical talent, strong research community, and long history of technology innovation make it a natural plac\",\"reading_time\":2,\"access\":true,\"comments\":false,\"og_image\":null,\"og_title\":null,\"og_description\":null,\"twitter_image\":null,\"twitter_title\":null,\"twitter_description\":null,\"meta_title\":null,\"meta_description\":null,\"email_subject\":null,\"frontmatter\":null,\"feature_image_alt\":null,\"feature_image_caption\":null},{\"_id\":\"6e4de399-b220-4678-ab12-57fa5f175e0f\",\"authorReferences\":[{\"_createdAt\":\"2026-10-05T14:49:30Z\",\"_id\":\"029f2c6c-6293-467a-925a-b8cf838434b9\",\"_rev\":\"t1Wu07esURumYhWaUauzyy\",\"_type\":\"blogAuthor\",\"_updatedAt\":\"2026-10-05T14:51:22Z\",\"fullName\":\"Mehrnaz Mofakhami\",\"language\":\"en-US\",\"profileImage\":{\"file\":{\"_type\":\"image\",\"asset\":{\"_ref\":\"image-1714edf59d26efe78a94f3a31611606253dd1599-504x502-png\",\"_type\":\"reference\"}},\"isHighFetchPriority\":false},\"slug\":{\"_type\":\"slug\",\"current\":\"blog/authors/mehrnaz-mofakhami\"},\"translatedUsingAI\":false},{\"_createdAt\":\"2026-03-20T18:38:07Z\",\"_id\":\"R1OZ62FO6FHW2RfkFCfafr\",\"_rev\":\"9CTItyT3Ypx2CnWeTdXGhW\",\"_type\":\"blogAuthor\",\"_updatedAt\":\"2026-06-15T20:04:22Z\",\"bio\":\"Senior Research Scientist, Cohere\",\"fullName\":\"Julia Kreutzer\",\"ghostSlug\":\"julia-kreutzer\",\"language\":\"en-US\",\"profileImage\":{\"_type\":\"object\",\"altText\":\"Portrait of Julia Kreutzer\",\"file\":{\"_type\":\"image\",\"asset\":{\"_ref\":\"image-fce8cda8535c91a16576e0b8cf7ad789f847b393-200x200-jpg\",\"_type\":\"reference\"}}},\"slug\":{\"_type\":\"slug\",\"current\":\"blog/authors/julia-kreutzer\"}},{\"_createdAt\":\"2026-03-20T18:38:54Z\",\"_id\":\"fwHcnm3fZ4C6IEnpDqR1ZJ\",\"_rev\":\"yk30mra3vRiFqmTUrihlj2\",\"_system\":{\"base\":{\"id\":\"fwHcnm3fZ4C6IEnpDqR1ZJ\",\"rev\":\"N5SHzfoaiQpafFvLXAdkfx\"}},\"_type\":\"blogAuthor\",\"_updatedAt\":\"2026-07-14T14:45:27Z\",\"bio\":\"Operations and Community Manager, Cohere Labs\",\"fullName\":\"Madeline Smith\",\"ghostSlug\":\"madeline\",\"language\":\"en-US\",\"profileImage\":{\"_type\":\"object\",\"altText\":\"Portrait of Madeline Smith\",\"file\":{\"_type\":\"image\",\"asset\":{\"_ref\":\"image-bced566137eecff1e965044918df1ca37453f8b2-512x512-png\",\"_type\":\"reference\"}}},\"slug\":{\"_type\":\"slug\",\"current\":\"blog/authors/madeline-smith\"}},{\"_createdAt\":\"2026-06-02T21:39:35Z\",\"_id\":\"4059ce5f-4dc3-4e4b-ba23-1c61bf5ac858\",\"_rev\":\"9CTItyT3Ypx2CnWeTdXVxE\",\"_type\":\"blogAuthor\",\"_updatedAt\":\"2026-06-15T20:06:45Z\",\"bio\":\"Head of Cohere Labs\",\"fullName\":\"Marzieh Fadaee\",\"language\":\"en-US\",\"profileImage\":{\"file\":{\"_type\":\"image\",\"asset\":{\"_ref\":\"image-596363683399a0f20f32060225d6acfe4b7e6267-400x400-jpg\",\"_type\":\"reference\"}},\"isHighFetchPriority\":false},\"slug\":{\"_type\":\"slug\",\"current\":\"blog/authors/marzieh-fadaee\"}}],\"body\":[{\"_key\":\"ab8344d751fa\",\"_type\":\"image\",\"alt\":\"Before/After diagram showing Arabic, Hindi, Swahili, Portuguese, Welsh languages detour through English vs. kept through reasoning \",\"asset\":{\"_ref\":\"image-21cf3c78f41276deb0647d65936e3ed260548f5f-3141x1218-png\",\"_type\":\"reference\"},\"horizontalAlignment\":\"items-center\",\"size\":\"lg\"},{\"_key\":\"0d0b1ae1979b\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"9ece6135f81e\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"The language of reasoning matters\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"h6\"},{\"_key\":\"7149827898c3\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"bb3bf9aad10c\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"Some of the biggest recent gains in language models have come from a simple idea: let the model ‘think’ before it answers. The result of this idea is the development of reasoning models that work through a problem step by step, producing a written ‘reasoning trace’. This has led to great improvements in language models’ abilities to complete tasks like math and coding. These models, however, mainly focus on giving the right answer, but we ask an often-overlooked question: can users actually follow the reasoning traces?\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"c34af980f7ca\",\"_type\":\"image\",\"alt\":\"Card for Command A Reasoning showing AI prompt, reasoning trace, final answer \",\"asset\":{\"_ref\":\"image-5f2548e59c4dfe20283e850df23ed599adc5200e-3732x2433-png\",\"_type\":\"reference\"},\"horizontalAlignment\":\"items-center\",\"size\":\"lg\"},{\"_key\":\"3b1295839ddd\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"08084ade11dc\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"For many users, the answer today is no: when a question is asked in Spanish, Arabic, or Swahili, these models tend to reason in English. The final answer may come back in the user's language, but the reasoning steps behind that answer stay out of reach for anyone who doesn't speak English. This is not an unsolvable problem. By enabling multilingual reasoning models to reason in the user’s language, which we call ‘\"},{\"_key\":\"f94516ea7c74\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"em\"],\"text\":\"in-language\"},{\"_key\":\"40bc6d05427e\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"’ or ‘\"},{\"_key\":\"878a6656792f\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"em\"],\"text\":\"L2\"},{\"_key\":\"8b6cf7a49006\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"’ reasoning, users can read the reasoning trace, verify each step, find where things went wrong when the answer is incorrect, and step in when needed.\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"0c182187bb8a\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"83e6263e5538\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"L2 reasoning may also help the model itself. Much knowledge, such as cultural context and linguistic nuance, is expressed most naturally in its source language. L2 reasoning could let models draw on this culturally embedded knowledge more effectively, especially in linguistic and open-ended generation tasks. The example below shows what this looks like in practice: Asking in Hindi which event signals good luck, our Tiny Aya L2-Thinker model reasons in Hindi about what Indian superstitions hold and reaches the correct answer, while thinking in English asserts a Western-biased answer without weighing the options.\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"0cb880b4cc99\",\"_type\":\"image\",\"alt\":\"Hindi and English reasoning traces for a superstition question, showing cultural anchors and predicted answers\",\"asset\":{\"_ref\":\"image-bc9b07ba79cb39e52e6d7cd4c221e766c47d8621-2460x1466-png\",\"_type\":\"reference\"},\"horizontalAlignment\":\"items-center\",\"size\":\"lg\"},{\"_key\":\"33fb54384fdc\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"6e6436991be4\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"In this blog post, we describe our recent research that shows how better data mixing leads to effective L2 reasoning without hurting accuracy much. Building on the \"},{\"_key\":\"ab81141971e4\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"cadc85053765\"],\"text\":\"Tiny Aya base model with 32K context length\"},{\"_key\":\"84ca385314cd\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\", we apply our data mixing strategy to create \"},{\"_key\":\"33aeff79f211\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"strong\"],\"text\":\"Tiny Aya L2-Thinker\"},{\"_key\":\"fdf164acfd11\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\", a massively multilingual model achieving over 93% in-language reasoning rate across 60 languages.\"}],\"markDefs\":[{\"_key\":\"cadc85053765\",\"_type\":\"link\",\"href\":\"https://huggingface.co/CohereLabs/tiny-aya-base-32K\"}],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"b98b085e9f3e\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"9c9b7e8ee85d\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"What’s missing from prior work\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"h6\"},{\"_key\":\"1e395a651c9b\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"3ee6e81c16d4\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"Getting models to reason in the user's language is not a new idea, but earlier attempts have fallen short in three ways. First, they show that L2 reasoning often comes at a cost: models that are pushed to reason in another language \"},{\"_key\":\"2636bcbd844c\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"5aabca2a881b\"],\"text\":\"tend to get more answers wrong\"},{\"_key\":\"c0d2ddf20d00\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\", and approaches that avoid this drop \"},{\"_key\":\"7b73f13f298a\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"9e67ae335dd1\"],\"text\":\"rely on complex, expensive training\"},{\"_key\":\"33e538987aca\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\". Second, most studies evaluate models only on math and science problems. Math is a good test of reasoning, but it's also the task where language matters least, so it tells us little about cultural knowledge or open-ended writing. Third, they cover only a handful of languages, usually ones that already receive a large amount of attention from AI researchers and developers.\"}],\"markDefs\":[{\"_key\":\"5aabca2a881b\",\"_type\":\"link\",\"href\":\"https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-emnlp.1103/\"},{\"_key\":\"9e67ae335dd1\",\"_type\":\"link\",\"href\":\"https://aclanthology.org/2026.acl-long.951/\"}],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"f034e6c4d13f\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"061b4901dc27\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"Our model, Tiny Aya L2-Thinker, addresses all three of these shortcomings: it reasons in the user's language while staying nearly as accurate as reasoning in English across a range of tasks, with the exception of competition-level math. We test it on math reasoning, commonsense understanding, cultural reasoning, open-ended writing, and instruction following. It also supports 45 languages in training, with evidence that it can carry this ability over to languages it was never shown reasoning examples in.\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"5806a535a5db\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"f4edf505a637\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"We also found that inference-time language forcing approaches don't close these gaps: Asking Qwen3.5-4B to think in the user's language barely changes what it does, and forcing the issue harder, by prefilling the opening words of its reasoning trace, works better but costs accuracy and isn't something most users can do. Reliable in-language reasoning has to come from training, which leaves the question of what to train on, the subject we focus on next.\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"aa5d744eb9db\",\"_type\":\"image\",\"alt\":\"Scatter plot comparing Tiny Ayas L2-Thinker and En-Thinker to larger models on task and reasoning rate\",\"asset\":{\"_ref\":\"image-286ec7c86a2740d3ad4f78e31ffca3c5be98b505-3564x2409-png\",\"_type\":\"reference\"},\"horizontalAlignment\":\"items-center\",\"size\":\"lg\"},{\"_key\":\"86f32a86af67\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"893cd2b3ee88\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"Optimizing data for transfer of reasoning beyond English\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"h6\"},{\"_key\":\"d06f8fe662de\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"a6c6779679e6\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"Our approach centers on the training data. How can we design training data so that the model learns to reason in languages other than English? We combine three types of data, which we call pillars, each teaching the model something different.\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"a042f1a4948f\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"b212be6664a6\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"The first pillar is \"},{\"_key\":\"74bedfae251e\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"strong\"],\"text\":\"English reasoning data (ER)\"},{\"_key\":\"7857cbc9b2e3\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\": about 1.7 million examples of step-by-step solutions generated by a larger model, gpt-oss-120b. This is where the model learns how to solve problems, and it matters most for math. On its own, though, it produces a model that almost always thinks in English: it reasons in the user's language only 12.8% of the time, even when overtly instructed to do so.\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"92af4ce05bb0\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"8cfeb4085689\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"The second pillar is \"},{\"_key\":\"236f950d3d05\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"strong\"],\"text\":\"multilingual reasoning data (MR)\"},{\"_key\":\"1288aa42a8cd\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\": we automatically translate English reasoning examples in math, science, and general topics into 44 languages. Translating long reasoning traces is costly, so we have only about 5,000 examples per language, a tiny amount compared to the English data. Yet this small set is enough to teach the model to reason in the user's language: adding it raises that rate from 12.8% to 86.1%, and accuracy improves too.\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"33d069d25fe0\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"d94d7abc24b3\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"The third and important pillar is \"},{\"_key\":\"d31b872b29f9\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"strong\"],\"text\":\"multilingual non-reasoning data (NR)\"},{\"_key\":\"1f107fc57a6a\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\": samples with questions and answers in the target language, but no reasoning traces. This data has historically been much more available because it has been used in prior multilingual instruction models. This pillar helps the model carry its ability to reason in the user's language over to new languages, including ones it never saw reasoning examples in.\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"e35a00c7956e\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"dd054843699f\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"The chart below shows how each data pillar builds on the last and progressively improves both L2 reasoning rate and accuracy. For a detailed analysis of each pillar and how we chose the final data mix, see \"},{\"_key\":\"4e51b48f5e32\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"c30b0d5fba16\"],\"text\":\"our paper\"},{\"_key\":\"bc8628cf2e26\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\".\"}],\"markDefs\":[{\"_key\":\"c30b0d5fba16\",\"_type\":\"link\",\"href\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2609.10445\"}],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"756a8f0ba9d9\",\"_type\":\"image\",\"alt\":\"Bar chart: ER (36.7%, 12.8%), ER+MR (45.3%, 86.1%), ER+MR+NR (57.4%, 96.4%) accuracy and reasoning rates\",\"asset\":{\"_ref\":\"image-9545b79e9d7ef694689e96a3b3ac5a8f4c8c2631-8451x3269-png\",\"_type\":\"reference\"},\"horizontalAlignment\":\"items-center\",\"size\":\"lg\"},{\"_key\":\"9ba680209cb2\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"660f332e7f60\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"What we measured, and what we found\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"h6\"},{\"_key\":\"f10ef6520917\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"2f6fcf9b3bc9\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"Beyond the accuracy scores that reasoning benchmarks usually report, we pay particular attention to three aspects we consider key to making reasoning models globally accessible:\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"1568696e1777\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"cda7d037d439\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"Are we achieving L2 reasoning without trading off quality?\"}],\"level\":1,\"listItem\":\"number\",\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"896b11f66cba\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"2c09617b1d76\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"Does performance hold up in lower-resource languages?\"}],\"level\":1,\"listItem\":\"number\",\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"dc13319fe4e0\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"f1599d36504e\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"Is the reasoning itself any good?\"}],\"level\":1,\"listItem\":\"number\",\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"0cfc0b794d3c\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"16aa29a9a24e\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"We test on six benchmarks across 60 languages, covering math (\"},{\"_key\":\"de2bdecd4431\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"26286f208e15\"],\"text\":\"MGSM\"},{\"_key\":\"08b607187624\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\" and \"},{\"_key\":\"d9f0ab5ac47e\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"130646583128\"],\"text\":\"PolyMath\"},{\"_key\":\"449eabe78a37\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"), cultural and commonsense reasoning (\"},{\"_key\":\"50b8cb3d0871\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"0733f3007d55\"],\"text\":\"Macaron‑MCQ\"},{\"_key\":\"1539a4a9dcde\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\" and \"},{\"_key\":\"0e4f8da92279\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"9d3d3ff932cb\"],\"text\":\"GlobalPIQA\"},{\"_key\":\"329fb0604336\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"), instruction following (\"},{\"_key\":\"23ce643ae49c\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"e692fba786b8\"],\"text\":\"Marco‑Bench‑MIF\"},{\"_key\":\"31810a837776\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"), and open-ended writing (\"},{\"_key\":\"5339def6c38b\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"99156b105da5\"],\"text\":\"MIST‑OEG\"},{\"_key\":\"0dac4d82ae48\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\").\"}],\"markDefs\":[{\"_key\":\"26286f208e15\",\"_type\":\"link\",\"href\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.03057\"},{\"_key\":\"130646583128\",\"_type\":\"link\",\"href\":\"https://www.arxiv.org/abs/2504.18428\"},{\"_key\":\"0733f3007d55\",\"_type\":\"link\",\"href\":\"https://www.arxiv.org/abs/2602.10732\"},{\"_key\":\"9d3d3ff932cb\",\"_type\":\"link\",\"href\":\"https://www.arxiv.org/abs/2510.24081\"},{\"_key\":\"e692fba786b8\",\"_type\":\"link\",\"href\":\"https://www.arxiv.org/abs/2507.11882\"},{\"_key\":\"99156b105da5\",\"_type\":\"link\",\"href\":\"https://aclanthology.org/2025.wmt-1.24/\"}],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"87554521540c\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"a728a9f7a14a\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"em\",\"strong\"],\"text\":\"Are we achieving L2 reasoning without trading off quality?\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"86da012c30db\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"c37fca84870d\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"This is the trade-off earlier work ran into, so it's the first thing to check. We compare Tiny Aya L2-Thinker against its own twin: Tiny Aya En-Thinker that was trained on the same base model and underlying data, but reasons in English.\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"6d0a5afa708e\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"ed5ef23c4103\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"Alongside accuracy, we measure the L2 reasoning rate: the share of responses where the model actually reasoned in the target language, which we determine by running a language identification model over each reasoning trace. A model can score well on accuracy while failing here completely, and that gap is exactly what we set out to close.\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"2ae673a28dc7\",\"_type\":\"image\",\"alt\":\"Bar chart comparing Tiny Ayas En-Thinker and L2-Thinker on task accuracy and L2 reasoning across benchmarks\",\"asset\":{\"_ref\":\"image-38182dc8659f3a3183dfa75bfa69682090507dfd-4567x1903-png\",\"_type\":\"reference\"},\"horizontalAlignment\":\"items-center\",\"size\":\"lg\"},{\"_key\":\"18c605827391\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"8a57093d137c\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"Accuracy barely moves. On five of six benchmarks it drops by at most two to three points, and on MIST-OEG, our open-ended writing benchmark, it actually goes up slightly. English performance holds up too: the model still reasons in English on English prompts and matches the English reasoner on most benchmarks. In exchange, the share of traces written in the user's language goes from near zero to above 93%.\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"ea0547971dc3\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"7978103af744\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"The exception is PolyMath, a competition-level math benchmark with problems well beyond grade-school arithmetic. Here accuracy falls more noticeably. We suspect this is because our model never went through a reinforcement learning stage, which hard math benefits from most and which could also smooth over artifacts introduced when we translated our training traces; this is a future direction that could be pursued with a focus on mathematics.\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"80f08ad7ae21\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"aaa7d592b27c\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"strong\",\"em\"],\"text\":\"Does performance hold up in lower-resource languages?\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"d11f2a86b65d\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"fc50e1d4fae4\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"In-language reasoning is typically harder to achieve for languages with less data available for training. We observe this when we compare to existing larger models built for L2 reasoning: M-Thinker-7B and Magistral-Small-24B. In the figure below we show how often each model reasons in the user's language, how accurate it is, and how many tokens it spends on reasoning as a measure of efficiency, across four tiers of resourcedness. The evaluation languages are grouped into tiers by how much web text exists for them, tier 1 being the highest-resource (e.g., English, German, French) and tier 4 the lowest (e.g., Swahili, Zulu, Welsh).\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"0ee5194f969b\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"ba872eec7e2b\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"Tiny Aya L2-Thinker is the only model that stays strong on all three axes as resource availability declines. Its L2 reasoning rate sits near the ceiling on tiers 1 and 2 (99%) and drops only slightly on the lowest two (94.3% and 94.5%). In contrast, Magistral collapses from 72% to 5% L2 reasoning rate, M-Thinker degrades on every axis and its traces roughly double in length, and language-forced Qwen keeps its L2 reasoning rate fairly high, but at the cost of accuracy and efficiency.\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"46581391e82c\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"dac5959ac267\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"Across the board, our model is also very economical, averaging under 5,000 thinking tokens, thanks to \"},{\"_key\":\"4877c29e06a3\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"3e75f079a02b\"],\"text\":\"its multilingually optimized tokenizer\"},{\"_key\":\"23d4282be71a\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\".\"}],\"markDefs\":[{\"_key\":\"3e75f079a02b\",\"_type\":\"link\",\"href\":\"https://cohere.com/blog/cohere-labs-tiny-aya\"}],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"9e242fb3b31f\",\"_type\":\"image\",\"alt\":\"Bar charts comparing Tiny Ayas L2-Thinker to other models across language tiers; higher reasoning, accuracy, fewer tokens\",\"asset\":{\"_ref\":\"image-4bf6086f6326f4d42f00395748bed6acc9f3ccfe-6264x2521-png\",\"_type\":\"reference\"},\"horizontalAlignment\":\"items-center\",\"size\":\"lg\"},{\"_key\":\"c794ada25fad\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"a5f9837cf447\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"strong\",\"em\"],\"text\":\"Avoiding typical reasoning failure modes: Is the reasoning itself any good?\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"b2712b9b993a\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"3af908113cf0\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"A model that thinks longer should, in principle, be “thinking harder”, irrespective of the language. Harder problems deserve more deliberation, and more tokens can mean more careful work. But across the models we tested, long reasoning traces usually meant something else: the model was going in circles.\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"142c0ef02222\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"db6ccbb6680c\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"We measure this with a repetition score, how often short sequences of text repeat within a trace, and compare it to how long the reasoning runs, shown in the scatterplot below. The two rise together: When models reason for longer they usually have a higher repetition rate. Reading the traces makes the pattern concrete: Many aren't long because the model is exploring alternatives; they're long because it is cycling through the same few steps, over and over, until it runs out of its token budget and stops without ever giving an answer. This is usually referred to as \\\"doomlooping\\\".\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"c333a9f05ba3\",\"_type\":\"image\",\"alt\":\"Scatter plot: thinking length vs 4-gram repetition; Tiny Ayas L2-Thinker balances length and low repetition\",\"asset\":{\"_ref\":\"image-3d22f1c64179714f5c2407399217b31ecff6f1c0-3116x1612-png\",\"_type\":\"reference\"},\"horizontalAlignment\":\"items-center\",\"size\":\"lg\"},{\"_key\":\"6e2f9561b3a0\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"a6ffda107b78\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"Qwen3.5-4B is the clearest case: it routinely spends 10,000 to 25,000 tokens reasoning that don’t necessarily improve performance. Prefilling its reasoning in the target language doesn't rescue it: language-forced Qwen remains in the same high-token, high-repetition corner, and as we saw in the language tiers plot above, it gets much less efficient on lower-resourced languages.\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"9a35cb4e7923\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"eff701cb65b5\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"Tiny Aya L2-Thinker mostly avoids this. On most benchmarks, it reasons in a few hundred to a few thousand tokens, with little repetition, and is also efficient on all language tiers, even low-resourced ones. Efficient reasoning is not solved yet, but it doesn’t have to be traded away for in-language reasoning.\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"779d00e50f54\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"153309ba6460\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"Summary and outlook\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"h6\"},{\"_key\":\"00d0cab3a0df\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"c0684490c1fe\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"We have shown that reasoning ability and reasoning language are separable. A model can learn to solve problems largely from English reasoning data, learn which language to reason in from a small amount of multilingual reasoning data, and generalize that behavior to new languages with multilingual non-reasoning data.\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"96ffdce6009e\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"52bc6c14af02\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"Reasoning in the right language, however, is only part of the picture. A reasoning trace can be understandable to users, but still loop, ramble, never arrive at a solution, or not actually meaningfully influence the solution. Further work should be done to make reasoning traces more meaningful, efficient and interpretable to downstream users.\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"641c91e7c4c2\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"af4486cafde5\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"To support further research on accessible, in-language reasoning, we release our model and multilingual reasoning data on Hugging Face.\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"c1695e1cf866\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"9d4bba945353\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"82ae4a6dc625\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"8da016af0223\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"Models:\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"5495e21b3633\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"b59d9b26da31\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"Tiny Aya L2-Thinker: \"},{\"_key\":\"ac37afd4ce4c\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"c6bb20fc0547\"],\"text\":\"https://huggingface.co/CohereLabs/tiny-aya-l2-thinker\"}],\"markDefs\":[{\"_key\":\"c6bb20fc0547\",\"_type\":\"link\",\"href\":\"https://huggingface.co/CohereLabs/tiny-aya-l2-thinker\"}],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"0b29b7ded9b0\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"cb1886dda64a\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"Tiny Aya En-Thinker: \"},{\"_key\":\"22d5cd038c08\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"7605c00663c7\"],\"text\":\"https://huggingface.co/CohereLabs/tiny-aya-en-thinker\"}],\"markDefs\":[{\"_key\":\"7605c00663c7\",\"_type\":\"link\",\"href\":\"https://huggingface.co/CohereLabs/tiny-aya-en-thinker\"}],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"374dbe82ca95\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"b14f96429fed\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"Tiny Aya Base 32K:\"},{\"_key\":\"a2b7f5cf5e68\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"a01f4d06dbe1\"],\"text\":\" https://huggingface.co/CohereLabs/tiny-aya-base-32K\"}],\"markDefs\":[{\"_key\":\"a01f4d06dbe1\",\"_type\":\"link\",\"href\":\"https://huggingface.co/CohereLabs/tiny-aya-base-32K\"}],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"06538bcaa7ec\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"c0fa4c6ef7b0\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"9eb4a39ce409\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"5da71e0ecea9\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"Try L2-Thinker on CohereLabs Space: \"},{\"_key\":\"28f59e559560\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"9dc37ceb2751\"],\"text\":\"https://huggingface.co/spaces/CohereLabs/tiny-aya-l2-thinker\"}],\"markDefs\":[{\"_key\":\"9dc37ceb2751\",\"_type\":\"link\",\"href\":\"https://huggingface.co/spaces/CohereLabs/tiny-aya-l2-thinker\"}],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"39529b65c6f9\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"ba78d3687021\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"99681ddd88c4\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"794dd731df04\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"Multilingual Reasoning data: \"},{\"_key\":\"c4d9c3fd55af\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"1e8999156865\"],\"text\":\"https://huggingface.co/datasets/CohereLabs/tiny-aya-l2-thinker-multilingual-reasoning\"}],\"markDefs\":[{\"_key\":\"1e8999156865\",\"_type\":\"link\",\"href\":\"https://huggingface.co/datasets/CohereLabs/tiny-aya-l2-thinker-multilingual-reasoning\"}],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"c9a814f54f61\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"b17da73a3c84\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"em\"],\"text\":\"For the full analysis, read the paper \"},{\"_key\":\"689cd5734b1b\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"bbc70b549227\",\"em\"],\"text\":\"here\"},{\"_key\":\"f2e985fc0149\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"em\"],\"text\":\".\"}],\"markDefs\":[{\"_key\":\"bbc70b549227\",\"_type\":\"link\",\"href\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2609.10445\"}],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"38c7401db7cd\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"5d8b0e20b613\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"If you use the model, the data, or find this work useful, please cite the paper:\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"d83e7955dd87\",\"_type\":\"codeBlock\",\"code\":\"@misc{mofakhami2026buildingmultilingualbridgesdata,\\n      title={Building Multilingual Bridges: Data Mixing as the Pillar of Generalization for In-Language Reasoning}, \\n      author={Mehrnaz Mofakhami and Ananya Sahu and Alejandro R. Salamanca and Daniel D'souza and Alexandre Berard and Thomas Euyang and Marzieh Fadaee and Julia Kreutzer},\\n      year={2026},\\n      eprint={2609.10445},\\n      archivePrefix={arXiv},\\n      primaryClass={cs.CL},\\n      url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.10445}, \\n}\",\"language\":\"python\"},{\"_key\":\"59429043a969\",\"_type\":\"divider\",\"style\":\"default\"},{\"_key\":\"cb8f94dc4cac\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"e6cce107f68b\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"We would like to thank everyone who contributed to this work and shared their insights: Ananya Sahu, Alejandro R. Salamanca, Daniel D'souza, Alexandre Berard, Thomas Euyang, David Stap, Maximilian Mozes, Kelly Marchisio, Ammar Khairi, Nikita Moghe, Sara Rajaee, Saurabh Dash, David Cairuz, Sammie Bae, Diana Abagyan, Björn Bebensee, Sylvie Shi and Pierre Beaulieu, and Aidan Peppin.\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"}],\"date\":\"2026-10-06T12:00:00.000Z\",\"heroImage\":{\"file\":{\"_type\":\"image\",\"asset\":{\"_ref\":\"image-b416a7d67e66515c45c29ca7b0664a6491ee9d5a-3840x2160-png\",\"_type\":\"reference\"}},\"isHighFetchPriority\":false},\"slug\":{\"_type\":\"slug\",\"current\":\"blog/building-multilingual-bridges\"},\"tagReferences\":[{\"_createdAt\":\"2026-03-17T21:41:11Z\",\"_id\":\"d13259d4-2457-408d-9da3-7f63541520dc\",\"_rev\":\"jtzXmr5sPTxAjhSjhwzUzL\",\"_system\":{\"base\":{\"id\":\"d13259d4-2457-408d-9da3-7f63541520dc\",\"rev\":\"ghCA0iPFLNDLy0OmrvmP3E\"}},\"_type\":\"blogTag\",\"_updatedAt\":\"2026-10-07T19:59:05Z\",\"emergingTopic\":true,\"language\":\"en-US\",\"slug\":{\"_type\":\"slug\",\"current\":\"blog/tag/research\"},\"tag\":[\"Research\"]}],\"title\":\"Multilingual Bridges: How Data Mixing Unlocks In-Language Reasoning\",\"url\":\"/blog/building-multilingual-bridges\"}]}]]}],\"$L38\"]}]\n38:[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"dir\":\"ltr\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"$L2c\",null,{\"footerNav\":\"$22:props:children:0:props:footerNav\",\"footerContacts\":\"$22:props:children:0:props:footerContacts\",\"ctaSectionTextAlign\":\"center\",\"portableBody\":\"$undefined\",\"portableHeadline\":\"$22:props:children:0:props:portableHeadline\",\"primaryCta\":\"$22:props:children:0:props:primaryCta\",\"secondaryCta\":\"$22:props:children:0:props:secondaryCta\",\"backgroundType\":\"image\",\"backgroundColor\":\"$undefined\",\"backgroundImage\":{\"desktopFile\":{\"_type\":\"image\",\"asset\":{\"_ref\":\"image-df2a70f280194c1fc4f01987c4c1b334618ad32a-2880x1200-png\",\"_type\":\"reference\"}},\"isHighFetchPriority\":false,\"mobileFile\":{\"_type\":\"image\",\"asset\":{\"_ref\":\"image-34119f5fc9acaa112cd70412d4c847045c917390-1232x928-png\",\"_type\":\"reference\"}},\"tabletFile\":{\"_type\":\"image\",\"asset\":{\"_ref\":\"image-df2a70f280194c1fc4f01987c4c1b334618ad32a-2880x1200-png\",\"_type\":\"reference\"}}},\"customBackgroundColor\":{\"label\":\"green\",\"value\":\"#355146\"},\"withCTASection\":true,\"withSimpleFooter\":\"$undefined\",\"currentYear\":2026,\"simpleFooterPrivacyLinks\":\"$undefined\",\"locales\":\"$5:props:value:localesConfig:locales\"}]}]\n"])</script></body></html>